WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.914
Hi! If you cut a paper box along its edges and lay it flat, what shape do you get? Today we'll learn about solids and their nets.

2
00:00:11.064 --> 00:00:19.818
A cube has 6 identical square faces. A cuboid also has 6 faces, all rectangles, though two of them can be squares.

3
00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:33.114
A triangular prism has a triangle at each end. A square pyramid has a square base and a pointed top. A cylinder has a circle at each end, and a cone has one circular base and a point.

4
00:00:33.264 --> 00:00:43.746
When drawing a cube or cuboid on isometric dot paper, keep the upright edges vertical. Hidden edges can be shown with dashed lines.

5
00:00:43.896 --> 00:00:47.970
Here is a net of a cube: 6 squares joined together.

6
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:54.690
Take the middle square as the base. Fold the four around it up, and fold the last one over as the lid.

7
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.266
It makes a cube, and no faces overlap.

8
00:00:58.416 --> 00:01:06.978
But 6 squares in one row is not a net. When you fold it, two faces overlap, and two faces are missing.

9
00:01:07.128 --> 00:01:13.626
A net of a triangular prism has 2 triangles and 3 rectangles: 5 faces in all.

10
00:01:13.776 --> 00:01:24.858
A net of a square pyramid has 1 square and 4 triangles, also 5 faces. When you see a net, count the shapes and you'll know which solid it makes.

11
00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:29.946
Watch out! Six joined squares are not always a net of a cube!

12
00:01:30.096 --> 00:01:38.274
In a row, folding only wraps four faces round in a loop. The two end squares overlap, and there's no top or bottom.

13
00:01:38.424 --> 00:01:51.978
To check, choose one square as the base and imagine folding the others up one by one. A cube needs a top, a bottom and four sides, so each of the six squares must end up on a different face.

14
00:01:52.128 --> 00:02:00.954
Your turn! How many faces does a net of a cuboid have? Pause and think.

15
00:02:01.104 --> 00:02:07.626
6, the same as the number of faces on a cuboid. Did you get it right?

16
00:02:07.776 --> 00:02:18.786
Another one! A net has 1 square and 4 triangles. Which solid does it make? Pause and think.

17
00:02:18.936 --> 00:02:27.714
A square pyramid. The square is the base, and the 4 triangles fold up to meet at the top. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:27.864 --> 00:02:38.178
Last one! 2 triangles and 3 rectangles. Which solid do they make? Pause and think.

19
00:02:38.328 --> 00:02:47.586
A triangular prism. The triangles are the two ends, and the rectangles wrap around the sides. Did you get it right?

20
00:02:47.736 --> 00:02:59.946
Remember: a net is a solid cut along its edges and laid flat. To check a net, count the shapes and how many there are, then pick a base and imagine folding to see if any faces overlap.

21
00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:05.226
Find an empty box, cut it open and look at its net!

